#{[17592186554563 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$"] [17592186554563 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=-12.$"] [17592186554565 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2)x+(-8)y+5z=8 \\\\ (-4)x+(-6)y+3z=-1 \\\\ 1x+0y+(-4)z=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -8 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186554570 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+1z=1 \\\\ 6x+(-2)y+(-1)z=-14 \\\\ 3x+1y+(-1)z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -14 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -14 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & -14 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-2+14)-3(6+42)+(6+6) \\\\  & =24-144+12 \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-108}{27}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186554573 :question/problem "$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}?$"] [17592186554573 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}=1+2=3.$"] [17592186554592 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=12-9k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4(-4-5)-2(-12-40)-4(-6+16) \\\\  & =36+104-40 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{100}{12-9k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=-\\frac{20}{3}.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{100}{12-9k} & =-\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ 15 & =-12+9k \\\\ 9k & =27 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186554598 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+(-8)y+3z=2 \\\\ 0x+6y+(-3)z=-7 \\\\ 1x+(-5)y+4z=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -10 & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186554616 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [17592186554616 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186555028 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186555037 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186555038 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186555052 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186555060 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186555069 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186555084 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-9t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186555092 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186555106 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186555142 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186555144 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186555147 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{9t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186555154 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186555167 :answer/value "